The tables in data/ and data-far/ are derived from works of the United States
Government, which are in the public domain:

  - the JPL planetary and lunar ephemerides DE440 and DE441 (Park, Folkner,
    Williams & Boggs, "The JPL Planetary and Lunar Ephemerides DE440 and
    DE441", The Astronomical Journal 161:105, 2021), NASA Jet Propulsion
    Laboratory;
  - the JPL Horizons orbit solutions for (2060) Chiron, (1) Ceres,
    (2) Pallas, (3) Juno and (4) Vesta.

Delta T is built from the IERS Earth orientation series (finals2000A.all,
published by the US Naval Observatory), the splines of Morrison, Stephenson,
Hohenkerk & Zawilski (2021) and the long-term parabola of Stephenson,
Morrison & Hohenkerk (2016); its uncertainty before 1700 follows Morrison &
Stephenson (2004). Precession, nutation and the fundamental arguments follow
the published IAU 2006 / IAU 2000B models as adopted by the IERS Conventions
(2003, 2010), and beyond 1550–2650 the long-term precession of Vondrák,
Capitaine & Wallace (Astronomy & Astrophysics 534, A22, 2011; corrigendum
541, C1, 2012).

This work contains no code or data from Swiss Ephemeris.
